sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because essentially all the kinds of systems studied (cellular automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, register machines and others) can emulate each other, a wide variety of systems with very different underlying structures are fundamentally equivalent in the computations they can perform, so computation can be studied abstractly with results applying across systems.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Structurally different systems are computationally equivalent";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 11, “The Notion of Computation”; section “Implications of Universality” (from p. 674), p. 674. Scope: cellular automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, register machines and other simple programs"
}
